   "l": "good shutting — no bolt, no bar, and yet it cannot be opened;\ngood knotting — no cord, no tie, and yet it cannot be undone.",
   "v": true,
   "n": "可 turns what follows passive with nothing else to mark it: 不可開 is 'cannot be opened', not 'cannot open'. You met the trick in the first line of the book, 道可道. 楗 is rare and unstable — many editions write 鍵, the metal pin. What holds the gate, on Wang Bi's reading, is not the hardware at all: the good shutter shuts by going with the nature of the thing, so there is nothing left to force."
  },
