   "l": "abidingly good at saving things,\nand so there are no thrown-away things.\nThis is called the carried-on light.\nSo: the good man,\nthe not-good man's teacher;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and always good at rescuing things, so that nothing is thrown away either. This is what is meant by carrying the light on. The good man, then, is the teacher of the man who is not good;",
   "n": "是謂 christens a term, so 襲明 is a coinage the reader should learn whole — and its sense is genuinely open. 襲 is a clothing word — to put one robe on over another — and from that, to take a thing over from someone and carry it forward: an heir 襲s a title. Read so, 襲明 is clarity handed on, kept in use rather than shown off, and that is what we print. Two other readings are old and live: 襲 as 'to cover over', the light kept hidden; and 襲 as 'to fall in behind, to follow', clarity that goes along with things as they are — which chimes with Wang Bi's 順自然, 'go along with what is so of itself', at the head of this chapter. The choice is not forced by anything in the commentary, so we make it on the lexical ground and say so rather than hide it. 者 is the nominaliser of chapter 15: 善人者 is 'the one who is good'."
  },
