   ],
   "l": "and the not-good man,\nthe good man's material.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and the man who is not good is the good man's stock to work on.",
   "n": "資 is a merchant's word: capital, provisions, the material laid in for a job. Wang Bi glosses it with 取, 'what is taken up' — the good man does not throw the bad man away, he draws on him. The pair 師 and 資, teacher and material, is the chapter's whole argument, and it is why the sage four lines above discards nobody: a world with no bad men in it would leave the good man nothing to work with."
  },
