   ],
   "l": "Not to prize one's teacher,\nnot to hold dear one's material —\nthough clever, greatly lost.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Fail to prize your teacher, fail to value your material, and clever as you are you have lost your road.",
   "n": "Three units of four characters, and nothing joins the first two to the third: English has to supply the 'and' or the 'then' that carries 不貴其師，不愛其資 into its consequence. Inside the third unit the join is made, and made by one character — 雖 'although', which is the whole of 'though clever, greatly lost'. 大 has been an adjective all through the book and is an adverb here; only its position says so. And 迷, losing the road, is set deliberately against 明, the clear sight named a few lines above — the chapter is built so that the two face each other."
  },
